some games use every aspect of their  world systems and mechanics to tell a  clever story the division 2 is not one  of those games as a loot shooter the  division 2 is a hell of a lot of fun the  combat is tight the grind is satisfying  and I love gaining control of the map  I'm about 20 hours in and I have no  intention of stopping soon I also adore  the game's aesthetic and world building  with overgrown streets and abandoned  national monuments you can almost smell  the pavement when a thunderstorm rolls  in during a firefight I prefer the  division ones wintery New York to this  new map but the latter is not lacking in  flavor what's disappointing is that  Ubisoft and massive entertainment don't  capitalize on the potential that world  building brings about that is to say  everything exists on a functional level  but not much more  NPCs don't seem like characters they  exist just to point me to the next fight  settlements are filled with survivors  yet somehow they feel utterly empty as I  explore the map and meet new people and  improve my character the remains of the  nation's capital become a concrete blur  at this point I'm sure many of you are  saying loot shooters do not need a story  and in the past I would have agreed many  people play these games for the  catharsis of the grind and the constant  flow of upgrades those are the main  reasons I played anthem and admittedly  the main reasons I'm enjoying the  division 2 but that kind of thinking can  only hold the genre back over the course  of the first Destiny's lifespan Bungie  gradually emphasized storytelling to  great effect it introduced new villains  flushed out existing characters and  overall made me give a [ __ ] about what I  was doing  destiny 2 has been playing narrative  ketchup for a while now but it is  catching up then there's warframe a game  in which space acrobatics and sci-fi  weapon crafting make way for one of the  biggest narrative twists and not just  loot shooters but modern video games  writ large warframe didn't need to tell  a story as it but it did and it is  monumentally better for it the division  twos narrative flaws are so glaring I  think because the foundation is here for  a great yarn take the Feder outpost for  example it's built on the roof of DC's  titular national theater spilling onto  these structures  baffling and alleyways on a purely  aesthetic level it's clever and I love  improving it over the course of my  playthrough it reinforces my sense of  progress it pushes the idea of  rebuilding the city and by extension the  United States but the majority of the  theaters inhabitants seem to exist only  to serve me the player whether as quest  givers or item vendors they just kind of  stand there  eagerly awaiting my return and the ones  that don't their lifeless husks an early  mission sets the stage for an emotional  wallop as an NPCs 19 year old daughter  has been kidnapped and naturally it's my  job to bring her back I won't spoil what  happens because there's not much to  spoil the aftermath contains all the  emotion of a department store mannequin  the very premise of this game is ripe  for narrative exploration you'll find  yourself fighting through museum  exhibits depicting the Vietnam conflict  and America's expansion West's  opportunities to confront the country's  blood-soaked past and maybe wrest  some sort of meaning from the ruins say  what you will about the first divisions  New York with its cardboard characters  and shotguns spray of a plot but that  game was grim it was oppressive we were  witnessing the collapse of the cultural  center of the world and massive drove  that idea home and as is the case with  many a looot shooter nowadays I'm sure  the developers will put more time and  brainpower into improving the division -  I'm just hoping many of those resources  will go into the narrative department  because more and more the games as  service field is becoming v Prime's onra  to tell a story through players spend  months often years returning to these  worlds they come for the grind and the  reliable thrills and they grow attached  so why not give them a story to talk  about as of now the division - wastes no  time telling me that humanity has lived  on after the collapse it wastes no time  telling me I should care about its  inhabitants but it spends far too little  time actually proving it  you
 

